{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CfNbLZamMUCnc7PKNw2e2","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"All four pages loaded (HTTP 200). The SR 26-2 cover letter does not cover several of the things you asked about, so I also fetched its attachment, and that attempt failed (explained under 1).\n\n**1. Fed SR 26-2**, https://www.federalreserve.gov/supervisionreg/srletters/SR2602.htm\n- It is titled \"Revised Guidance on Model Risk Management\" and dated **April 17, 2026**. It is signed by Randall D. Guynn, Director, Division of Supervision and Regulation.\n- It is issued jointly by the Federal Reserve Board, the OCC and the FDIC (\"the agencies\").\n- **It replaces both earlier letters.** It \"supersedes and replaces SR letter 11-7\" (Guidance on Model Risk Management, April 4, 2011) and \"SR letter 21-8\" (Interagency Statement on Model Risk Management for Bank Systems Supporting BSA/AML Compliance, April 9, 2021).\n- **Who it applies to:** it is \"expected to be most relevant to banking organizations with over $30 billion in total assets regulated by the Federal Reserve.\"\n- **Why it was revised:** it reflects \"supervisory experience and industry feedback accumulated over the past fifteen years, as well as significant advancements in modeling practices.\"\n- **Main theme:** it emphasises \"a risk-based approach to model risk management that is tailored to a banking organization's model risk profile and the size and complexity of its operations.\" It also says practices \"appropriately vary among banking organizations.\"\n- **Not on the cover page:** AI, generative AI or agentic models; inventory, validation, governance, board or senior management roles; internal audit; and any effective date separate from the issue date. That detail is in the attached guidance, https://www.federalreserve.gov/supervisionreg/srletters/SR2602a1.pdf. I fetched it (HTTP 200), but it came back as unreadable PDF binary, so I can't tell you what it says on those points. WebFetch saved the PDF as a local file, so it can be read directly.\n\n**2. OSFI Guideline E-23, Model Risk Management (2027)**, https://www.osfi-bsif.gc.ca/en/guidance/guidance-library/guideline-e-23-model-risk-management-2027\n- It was published September 11, 2025, and the **effective date is May 1, 2027**.\n- **Scope:** it covers \"all federally regulated financial institutions, including foreign bank branches and foreign insurance company branches.\" It applies on a risk basis, in proportion to size, strategy, risk profile, complexity and interconnectedness.\n- **AI/ML is explicitly in scope.** A model is defined as statistical and other techniques \"including AI/ML methods, which processes input data to generate results.\" AI is defined using the OECD definition, and the page says \"Models characterized by dynamic self-learning and autonomous decision-making may become prevalent.\"\n- **Three expected outcomes:** (1) \"Model risk is well understood and managed across the enterprise\"; (2) \"Model risk is managed using a risk-based approach\"; (3) \"Model governance covers the entire model lifecycle.\"\n- **Inventory:** it is the institution's \"system of record\" and should contain \"all models whose inherent risk is determined to be non-negligible.\" It should be \"accurate, evergreen, and subject to robust controls,\" and should include vendor and third-party models and, for a period, decommissioned ones. Appendix 1 lists the minimum fields to track for each model.\n- **Lifecycle:** design (rationale, data, development), review, approval, deployment, monitoring and decommission. Model review must be \"independent from model development.\" Monitoring must handle \"autonomous decision making, autonomous re-parametrization, and the elevated potential for model drift.\"\n- **Accountability:** senior management defines MRM roles and accountabilities, ensures staff have the right skills \"particularly for novel technologies, like AI,\" and reports model risk to the board. The guideline defines the roles of model owner, developer, reviewer, approver and user.\n\n**3. OSFI Guideline B-13, Technology and Cyber Risk Management**, https://www.osfi-bsif.gc.ca/en/guidance/guidance-library/technology-cyber-risk-management\n- The page is dated July 31, 2022, and was last modified 2024-02-20. **It does not state an effective date.**\n- **Scope:** \"all federally regulated financial institutions (FRFIs), including foreign bank branches and foreign insurance company branches.\" It is meant to be applied on a risk basis.\n- It is organised into three domains: Governance and risk management; Technology operations and resilience; Cyber security.\n- **Outcome 1:** risks are \"governed through clear accountabilities and structures, and comprehensive strategies and frameworks.\" Senior management assigns responsibility to senior officers such as the CTO, CIO or CISO.\n- **Outcome 2:** \"A technology environment that is stable, scalable and resilient.\" This includes an up-to-date technology asset inventory, the system development life cycle (SDLC), change and patch management, incident management and a disaster recovery programme.\n- **Outcome 3:** \"A secure technology posture that maintains the confidentiality, integrity and availability\" of technology assets. Controls are grouped as identify, defend, detect, and respond/recover/learn, and include MFA, privileged access management and threat-led testing.\n- There are 17 numbered principles. It should be read together with other OSFI guidance, including E-21 and B-10.\n\n**4. EIOPA page on DORA**, https://www.eiopa.europa.eu/digital-operational-resilience-act-dora_en\n- DORA is \"a regulation introduced by the European Union to strengthen the digital resilience of financial entities.\"\n- **Regulation number:** \"Regulation (EU) 2022/2554 ... of 14 December 2022,\" alongside the amending Directive (EU) 2022/2556.\n- **Application date:** it \"entered into application on 17 Jan 2025.\"\n- **Who it covers:** it is \"applicable to 20 different types of financial entities and ICT third-party service providers.\"\n- **What it covers:** ICT risk management, ICT third-party risk management, resilience testing, ICT incident reporting, information sharing, and oversight of critical third-party providers.\n- It sets up \"an EU-wide oversight framework for critical ICT third-party providers (CTPPs)\" to deal with concentration risk.\n- The page lists the Level 2 rules (RTS/ITS on the risk management framework, incident reporting, threat-led penetration testing, subcontracting and the register of information) and Level 3 guidelines."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":30579,"cache_read_input_tokens":49414,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":30579,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":3,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01W8ynNsBg4bWkQaxTuYe1qR","session_id":"26f0686f-6b6a-4bca-80e3-d1af26120d40","uuid":"4600a75a-e4e3-4135-bd9b-a22b66b514be","timestamp":"2026-09-24T16:38:29.722Z","request_id":"req_011CfNbLZ7yWVaXboF49UAuH","subagent_type":"web-fetch","task_description":"Read SR 26-2 and OSFI/DORA sources"}
